  "body": "# Associated User Story\r\n<br>\r\nAs a member marking myself present for an event, I would like to use my phone’s browser and be able to navigate the website seamlessly, so I can easily mark attendence or track status on my phone without having a hard time navigating the web app.\r\n\r\n\r\n# Acceptance Criteria\r\n<br>\r\n**Members can mark attendance and check their status on a phone browser without difficulty**\r\n\r\n- Sunny: A member opens the site in their phone's browser (iPhone Safari or Android Chrome), logs in, scans the event QR code, gets marked present, and then opens their dashboard to check their points and status. Every page fits the screen, text is readable without zooming, and buttons are easy to tap.\r\n\r\n- Rainy: The member's phone has a small screen, is held in landscape, or is on a slow connection. The layout still adjusts with nothing cut off or overlapping, and a loading indicator or clear \"try again\" message appears instead of a frozen or blank page. If location permission is denied, the member sees a message explaining how to allow it, rather than a silent failure.\r\n\r\nDefinition of Done -\r\nA story is done when its acceptance criteria are met and every item in the team PR template is satisfied:\r\n(A) code review by a non-author, coding standard met, RuboCop checked with output/CI link, and setup changes documented;\r\n(B) the human-only maintainability checks (meaningful comments and names, skinny controllers, thin views, DRY, Rails conventions, foreign keys, enums, nested routes);\r\n(C) tests added or updated and passing. Any deferred items or RuboCop exclusions are explained in Section D of the PR.",
